; The Files drawer DIR names becomes the current directory: every name below is relative to it.
; DIR is spelled as the caller gave it, so it may be relative to the drawer the script was started
; from (the README's Shell line: CD up-term, then Execute Files/install.dos Files): after this CD it
; would name Files/Files. $uptkit is the same drawer as an absolute name. A later use of the drawer
; (a program started by its path, a script handed the drawer, a CD back after another one) goes
; through $uptkit, never through DIR.
; The AmigaDOS Shell (3.1 and 3.2): CD alone prints where the Shell stands. vsh (upkg runs every
; postinst under it): a bare CD there is bash's cd, it prints nothing (and does not move the script),
; so the empty value falls back to vsh's $PWD, which its CD above set. Installed vsh 1.1 behaves so
; too, which is why the fragment and not vsh handles it. Rig-tested both ways on OS 3.1 and 3.2.
CD "{DIR}"
Set uptkit "`CD`"
If "$uptkit" EQ ""
  Set uptkit "$PWD"
EndIf
